# PXA Overdrive -- recommended per-model preset for Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (PXQN2)
#
# Model : the Flash-Next PXQN2 file
# Cards : measured on 1x Tesla P100 16 GB (sm_60) + system RAM. A second card depends on the topology:
#         two P100 on the SAME NUMA node measured faster (39.22 / 40.48 prose / code vs one card's
#         25.18 / 26.08, same session and argv, 2026-10-12, layer split); two cards on DIFFERENT sockets (SYS link) are
#         unmeasured. Tensor split is impossible for this model.
#
# Since 2026-10-19 the Overdrive switch set is not written out in this file: the engine applies it itself at
# startup (PXA_AUTO_OVERDRIVE, on by default) when it reads this class out of the model file and the file is larger
# than the cards. The set and the decision that applies it live in the PXQN library, so on a build without that
# library nothing is applied. What each switch does, the mechanism it turns on and the measurement behind it, is
# documented with the licensed PXQN library. This file carries the one line that forces the set on a card the file
# would fit (PXA_OVERDRIVE=1), the box row an operator may have to adjust, and the site rows the numbers below were
# taken with. PXA_NO_OVERDRIVE=1 turns the set off; a value you export for any one switch is kept.
#
# The expert map: the expert cache wants to know which experts this model routes to. It looks for
# <model>.expert-counts.csv next to the model file, then in the per-user cache directory
# ($PXA_CACHE_DIR, else $XDG_CACHE_HOME/pxa, else ~/.cache/pxa). The model download ships this map
# beside its shards. If no map is found, the server profiles the model once on first start, writes
# the map and restarts itself -- one extra load, and nothing to configure. Without either, the
# engine streams whole layers of experts and runs several times slower on a card that cannot hold
# the model, so this preset does NOT hardcode a counts path.
#
# Measured 2026-10-09, one P100 16 GB + system RAM, greedy, 512 tokens, three runs per cell:
#   Overdrive off  26.91 prose / 32.28 code
#   Overdrive on   30.70 prose / 37.96 code      (+14% / +18%)
# The 2026-10-08 gate on the same model measured 29.52 / 37.81 with the set on, and the set was the winning arm of
# three arms tested. Depth caps 4 and 5, and the wide-verify/SC-5 pair, were each measured and left out (losses).
# Those numbers were taken with this preset's earlier arm (expert residency adapting); the set the engine applies
# is the frozen-residency recipe E, measured faster still on one V100 (+17% prose / +27% code over the earlier arm,
# 2026-10-14) and bit-for-bit repeatable there.
#
# Run it: pass this file to your container/host as environment (docker: --env-file, or source it),
# with the server arguments listed at the bottom of this file.

# --- Overdrive: the engine applies the measured switch set for this class; =1 forces it even on a card the file
# would fit (the class itself must match), =0 turns it off like PXA_NO_OVERDRIVE=1 ---
PXA_OVERDRIVE=1

# --- box ---
# the CPU pool for the cold-expert path: 16 CPUs on the NUMA node your card hangs off. 0-15 is that node
# on the box this was measured on; on an interleaved dual-socket box (node0 = 0,2,4,...) it names CPUs of
# BOTH sockets. PXA Control / pxa-launch derive it from your topology; set it by hand only without them.
PXA_XCACHE_CPUPOOL_CPUS=0-15

# --- site ---
GGML_CUDA_NO_PINNED=1
PXA_PLE_MMAP=2
CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID

# --- server arguments (as measured) ---
#   -ot 'per_layer_token_embd\.weight=CPU' -ngl 99 -fa on -np 1 -t 16 --jinja --no-context-shift
#   --cache-ram 0 -c 16384 -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 --spec-type mtp:n_max=3,p_min=0.75
#
# LEAVE OFF (measured, not chosen): depth cap 4 or 5 (-3..-4% code); PXA_CPU_POOL_PERSIST and
#   PXA_CUDA_GRAPH_WIDTHKEY (wash); PXA_CUDA_GRAPH_SLOTS (-9.5% prose); the cache-admission pair
#   (lossy); the wide-verify pair with a speculative guard (loses to the Overdrive set).
# On a machine with more than one CPU socket, a container needs --cap-add SYS_NICE for the memory
# policy (PXA_NUMA_BIND) to apply.
